<span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></span>
<span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></span>
<span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></span>
<span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></span>
<span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.12.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></p>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When collecting test data, we first extract diverse visual and auditory inputs from 37 datasets, including natural sounds, music, dialogue, and various images.
To evaluate voice personalization, we utilize texts from RoleBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023c</a>)</cite> and analyze both role-specific speaking style and vocal timbre, thereby demonstrating the model’s potential for personalized interactions. Specifically, we select 100 distinct roles and collect three representative audio clips per role.
To replace the original text instructions, we synthesize speech instructions using three advanced text-to-speech (TTS) models and evaluate the outputs with UTMOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saeki et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, regenerating any clips that receive a score lower than 3.8. The synthesized audio is then transcribed with Whisper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and compared to the original instructions. Only the audio recordings with the lowest word error rates are retained.
To ensure data quality and reliability, we conduct a multi-stage curation process. First, we de-duplicate the content using a combination of lexical overlap and edit-distance matching. Next, we filter out incomplete or inappropriate items, including cases with missing or broken audio or images, as well as instances with ill-posed questions or incorrect answers. Approximately 10% of candidates are removed during this stage. The remaining examples are mapped to 13 task categories, and we apply balanced sampling to equalize category representation. The final benchmark comprises roughly 10.5K carefully curated Q&amp;A items.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate 21 open-source models, as well as the advanced proprietary model GPT-4o-Audio, on <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span>, and some detailed scores are shown in Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (a). We report several key findings.
<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First</span>, proprietary models do not consistently outperform open-source ones. For example, GPT-4o-Audio fails to surpass open-source models in 4 out of 13 tasks.
<span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second</span>, current models tend to perform better on speaking than on listening. We observe that 20 out of 22 models score higher on Speaking than on Listening, and this mismatch highlights the need for more balanced development.
<span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Third</span>, smaller but well-designed models can rival or even exceed the performance of larger models. Notably, Step-Audio-2-mini achieves more than double the listening accuracy of the 32B LLaMA-Omni2 model (40.06 vs. 16.00) and obtains the highest overall score.
<span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fourth</span>, role-play tasks and multi-modal (vision + audio) integration remain challenging for current models. For example, Step-Audio achieves the highest content score (33.2) and speaker similarity (75.1), but has the second lowest Speech Naturalness (56.0). Qwen2.5-Omni-7B achieves 59.2% accuracy on image+text queries but only 42.9% on image+audio queries, reflecting a 16.3-point drop.
<span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finally</span>, safety alignment and robustness require further improvement. Some smaller or unaligned models, such as the Moshika family, perform poorly on both fronts (robustness below 1, safety below 28).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> is the first benchmark to provide a comprehensive quantitative and qualitative evaluation of AI assistants across listening, speaking, and viewing abilities.
Our aim with <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> is to push the boundaries of what AI assistants can achieve. We believe it will be instrumental in developing next-generation multimodal models and in tracking progress toward truly expert AI assistants.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speech Interaction Benchmarks.</span>
VoiceBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> benchmarks LLM-based voice assistants, but it evaluates only text responses, missing the speech-specific richness. Other benchmarks, VocalBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, SOVA-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, SD-Eval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and WildSpeech-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, focus on speech abilities, though SOVA-Bench still uses text instructions, limiting hands-free evaluation. All of these benchmarks emphasize simple conversations without audio/visual context or voice imitation. In contrast, <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> assesses listening, speaking, and viewing, covering personalized voice cloning, hands-free audio interaction, and multimodal understanding, better reflecting real-world assistant needs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Audio and Vision Understanding Benchmarks.</span>
Speech datasets include LibriSpeech <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panayotov et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, Common Voice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ardila et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, Clotho <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Drossos et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, AudioCaps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and AVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; for music, MusicCaps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agostinelli et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and MUSIC-AVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">li et al., 2022</a>)</cite> support captioning and QA tasks. Broader evaluations include SUPERB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, HEAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baur et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and AIR-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the first generative audio-language benchmark. In vision, MMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, MathVista <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and MathVision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> test multimodal reasoning, with MMMU spanning diverse disciplines. All, however, follow audio/image-in, text-out paradigms. By contrast, <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> is the first to evaluate models in rich audio–visual contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MultiModal Large Language Models.</span>
LLMs have expanded beyond text into audio, with speech-focused models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, sound and music systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gardner et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and general audio models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that handle mixed audio sources. In vision, multimodal LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> show strong perception but are tested on narrow tasks. Recently, omni-modal models such as GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Qwen2.5-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, MiniCPM-o-2.6 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and Baichuan-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> unify text, images, and audio. Yet, their performance on complex, multi-turn multimodal instructions remains untested.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S2.F2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:231pt;vertical-align:-113.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-1.6pt,0.9pt) scale(0.992209183801535,0.992209183801535) ;">
