</span></div>
</div>
        
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:190.8pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22651v1/pie_subsets.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:264/264;" width="264" height="264"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Proportional distribution of tasks and the corresponding weaknesses addressed in <span id="S2.F2.fig1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Dataset</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Data Collection</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> is designed to bridge the gaps identified in existing benchmarks. In particular, it follows these targeted guidelines: (1)
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Voice Personalization:</span> We include tasks that require the assistant to mimic specific speaker voices. By curating multiple voice profiles and personalized voice-generation tasks, we can evaluate the model’s ability to adapt its timbre and style to a given individual’s voice (addressing W1).
(2) <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hands-free Interaction:</span> All tasks use spoken prompts and require spoken responses, with no reliance on text-based instructions. This “voice-first” approach reflects real-world usage (e.g., driving, operating machinery, or assisting visually impaired users) and tests the model’s reliability in hands-free scenarios (addressing W2).
(3) <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diverse Audio Contexts:</span> The audio data span a wide range of everyday soundscapes, including natural sounds, music, human speech, and environmental noises. By incorporating noisy settings, overlapping sounds, and complex acoustic scenes, we ensure that the assistant is evaluated under realistic listening conditions (addressing W3).
(4) <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal Integration:</span> We combine auditory data with visual inputs in our tasks. For example, some tasks, such as intelligent tutoring, require answering questions about an image using both what is seen and what is heard. These audio-visual tasks test the assistant’s ability to jointly process speech and vision, reflecting real-world multimodal requirements (addressing W4).
Accordingly, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> is organized into three main subsets that reflect these principles:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Listening.</span>
This subset contains audio clips featuring a variety of sounds, music, human speech, and their combinations, often accompanied by realistic background noise. It is designed to evaluate the assistant’s ability to recognize and interpret sounds in diverse contexts. We collect source data from 23 audio understanding datasets, following the practice of AirBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which is the first general audio understanding benchmark. The collected text instructions are then converted into audio. Specifically, for the Speech subset, the sources are 10 datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panayotov et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Drossos et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poria et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bastianelli et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nagrani et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimao &amp; Tzerpos, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cieri et al., 2004</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Antoniou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; for Sound, the sources are 6 datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong &amp; Park, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mesaros et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipping et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; for Music, the sources are 4 datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bogdanov et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agostinelli et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; and for General, the sources are 3 datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ardila et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
Details can be found in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Source Datasets ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speaking.</span>
This subset comprises spoken-interaction tasks across multiple topics and real-world scenarios. All interactions are voice-driven, with a special emphasis on tasks that require the model to imitate a target speaker’s voice. This setup probes both voice personalization and performance in hands-free dialogues.
We pre-design eight sub-tasks: assistant, emotion, instruction following, multi-round, reasoning, robustness, role-playing, and safety.
For role-playing, we use the role profiles and question texts from RoleBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023c</a>)</cite>. Other source data are from 22 datasets, including 11 text datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talmor et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzgun et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib117" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib118" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and 11 audio datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Defferrard et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Livingstone &amp; Russo, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oord et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">James et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradhan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Details can be found in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Source Datasets ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Viewing.</span>
This subset contains a wide variety of images from different domains, used in conjunction with audio tasks (e.g., an image paired with a spoken question). It evaluates the assistant’s ability to integrate visual context with auditory information.
To ensure the Viewing subset covers as many image types as possible, we select MMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as the source dataset. MMMU spans 30 disciplines and 30 image formats, including diagrams, tables, charts, chemical structures, photos, paintings, geometric shapes, music sheets, medical images, and more.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Speech Synthesis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We synthesize speech for question texts using three advanced text-to-speech (TTS) models: F5TTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, ChatTTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2025</a>)</cite>, and Dia-1.6B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sung et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We preprocess the texts to ensure high synthesis quality:
1. Image references: We replace special tokens (e.g., “&lt;image1&gt;”) with descriptive phrases, such as “as shown in the first image.”
2. Text rewriting: We use Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to rewrite the text so it is more suitable for TTS, ensuring that challenging content, such as numbers and mathematical expressions, is read accurately. For example, “<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OC={2r\sin(\pi)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OC={2r\sin(\pi)}</annotation></semantics></math>” is converted to “O C equals two r times sine of pi,” and “$31,000” is converted to “thirty-one thousand dollars”.
3. Multiple-Choice Questions: We rephrase the options for better readability. For instance, the options “[Man, Woman]” are converted to “Man or Woman?”
During speech synthesis, we use UTMOS Strong <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saeki et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to evaluate the generated speech and regenerate it until the score surpasses 3.8. To increase speaker diversity, we reuse the 300 role audio clips from the role-play task as prompt audio. Finally, we use Whisper-Large-v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to transcribe the synthesized audio back into text and select the audio sample with the lowest word error rate.
In total, we synthesize 13,471 high-quality speech audio files.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Data curation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We improve data quality through a four-stage curation process:
(1) Deduplication: We use lexical overlap and Levenshtein distance to flag potential duplicate questions. Suspected duplicates are manually reviewed and removed, resulting in 15,359 samples.
(2) Validation: We exclude incomplete or unsuitable instances, including entries with corrupted images, silent audio, ill-posed prompts, and demonstrably incorrect answers. Approximately 10% of candidates are discarded at this stage. Further details can be found in Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Data Curation Tool ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>.
(3) Categorization: We assign the remaining problems to 13 predefined task categories, primarily based on their source metadata.
(4) Sampling: We sample data from the categorized problems to balance the ratio of different tasks. After this, we obtain 10,497 high-quality samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Protocals.</span>

Speech conveys rich information beyond what text alone can express. Unlike previous studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which focus solely on evaluating text responses, our approach offers a comprehensive assessment of both generated speech and text responses, as well as their consistency. To facilitate comparison and interpretation, we aggregate multiple detailed metrics into a single, unified score. This holistic evaluation distinguishes <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> from VocalBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which reports UTMOS and WER independently, making it challenging to obtain an integrated view of model performance across tasks. A thorough breakdown of model capabilities, including content, speech, and consistency, can be found in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Detailed Assessment of Model Capabilities ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Triadic Evaluation System.</span>
We evaluate model responses across three key dimensions: content quality, speech quality, and consistency between them. For content quality, we use gpt-oss-20b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to assess response texts with thirteen evaluator prompts, as shown in Appendix <a href="#A11.SS2" title="K.2 Evaluation Prompts ‣ Appendix K Experiment Details ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K.2</span></a>. For emotion, we use emotion2vec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to get the emotion probabilities for angry, disgusted, fearful, happy, neutral, sad, and surprised, and keep all the emotions with probabilities larger than 1% in the prompts. Using these prompts, the model judger identifies the essential elements of each task, compares the model outputs with reference answers, and delivers a final judgment.
To measure speech quality, we employ UTMOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saeki et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which provides an overall score reflecting fluency and naturalness. For evaluating consistency between text and speech modalities, we first transcribe the speech outputs using Whisper-Large-v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and then compute a modified Word Error Rate (WER) between the transcriptions and the model’s text responses.
For multiple-choice questions, if the model only outputs a final answer letter, the traditional WER may differ significantly from the actual situation. To address this, we introduce a length threshold, ensuring a more accurate evaluation of consistency. The final score for each task is calculated by multiplying the three individual scores and then converting the result to a percentage.</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Let }n=\operatorname{len}\bigl(c\in\mathrm{lower}(\text{text}_{1})\mid c\neq\texttt{space}\bigr),\quad m=\operatorname{len}\bigl(c\in\mathrm{lower}(\text{text}_{2})\mid c\neq\texttt{space}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Let </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>len</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>lower</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>text</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≠</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">space</mtext></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>len</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>lower</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>text</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≠</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">space</mtext></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Let }n=\operatorname{len}\bigl(c\in\mathrm{lower}(\text{text}_{1})\mid c\neq\texttt{space}\bigr),\quad m=\operatorname{len}\bigl(c\in\mathrm{lower}(\text{text}_{2})\mid c\neq\texttt{space}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{WER^{\prime}}(\text{text}_{1},\text{text}_{2})=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\min(n,m)&lt;10\text{ and }\max(n,m)&gt;10,\\[4.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{if }\min(n,m)&lt;10\text{ and }\max(n,m)\leq 10,\\[4.0pt]
\dfrac{\operatorname{Levenshtein}(\text{text}_{1},\text{text}_{2})}{\max(n,m)},&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>WER</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>text</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>text</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Levenshtein</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>text</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>text</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{WER^{\prime}}(\text{text}_{1},\text{text}_{2})=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\min(n,m)&lt;10\text{ and }\max(n,m)&gt;10,\\[4.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{if }\min(n,m)&lt;10\text{ and }\max(n,m)\leq 10,\\[4.0pt]
\dfrac{\operatorname{Levenshtein}(\text{text}_{1},\text{text}_{2})}{\max(n,m)},&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Role-play.</span>
For the role-play task, we use Wespeaker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite> to calculate the speaker similarity between the model-generated speech and the reference role audio. The speaker similarity score is then incorporated as a factor in the final score for the role-play task.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> across 22 models, including six that are capable of processing both audio and visual inputs. The 21 open-source models are categorized by size as follows:
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Small models (&lt;4B parameters):</span> Seven compact models, including mini-omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie &amp; Wu, 2024b</a>)</cite>, mini-omni2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie &amp; Wu, 2024a</a>)</cite>, and the LLaMA-Omni2 series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, along with Baichuan-Omni-1d5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medium-scale models ( 7B parameters):</span> Eleven models, including the Moshi variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Défossez et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Royer et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Llama-3.1-8B-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Freeze-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>, LLaMA-Omni2-7B-Bilingual <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, glm-4-voice-9b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">KimiTeam et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, MiniCPM-o-2_6 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Step-Audio-2-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Qwen2.5-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Large models (&gt;10B parameters):</span> Step-Audio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and the LLaMA-Omni2 series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
We also include proprietary GPT-4o-Audio in the evaluation.
We provide the hyperparameters used in Appendix <a href="#A11.SS1" title="K.1 Model Setup ‣ Appendix K Experiment Details ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Models’ scores on <span id="S4.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span>. Listening Tasks: GEN: General, MUS: Music, SND: Sound, SPH: Speech. Speaking Tasks: AST: Assistant, EMO: Emotion, IF: Instruction Following, MR: Multi-Round, RSN: Reasoning, RBT: Robustness, RLP: Roleplay, SFT: Safety. Viewing Task: MDS: Multi-Discipline. The highest scores among open-source models are highlighted in <span id="S4.T3.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">red</span>, the second-highest scores in <span id="S4.T3.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D2E6FA;">blue</span>, and the third-highest in <span id="S4.T3.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCF4E5;">green</span>.</figcaption>
