<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proprietary models cannot definitively surpass open-source models.</span>
The proprietary GPT-4o-Audio fails to outperform open-source models in 4 out of 13 tasks. Specifically, in the Listening Sound and Listening Speech tasks, there is a decrease compared to Step-Audio-2-mini of 4.3 (47.7 vs. 52.0) and 9.1 (37.4 vs. 46.5), respectively. Furthermore, GPT-4o-Audio achieves a score of only 74.5 in Speaking Safety, which is 5.3 points lower than Freeze-Omni. In contrast to its ordinary performance in safety tasks, GPT-4o-Audio demonstrates strong performance in Speaking Robustness (54.7), outperforming the best open-source model, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B (38.6), by 16.1 points.
GPT-4o-Audio primarily excels in tasks that require a robust LLM backbone, including Speaking Assistant (62.7), Speaking Instruction Following (44.3), Speaking Multiround (64.0), and Speaking Reasoning (63.8). This indicates that its superior performance is mainly inherited from its underlying LLM backbone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Current models tend to perform better on speaking tasks than on listening tasks.</span> Across the board, models perform substantially better on speaking-oriented tasks than on audio understanding.
In the high-level results shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, 20 out of 22 models score higher on Speaking than on Listening. This suggests that voice assistants may handle spoken commands and dialogues well, but still struggle to interpret non-speech audio, such as music or environmental sounds.
An exception is Step-Audio-2-mini, which scores 40.06 on listening and only 31.30 on Speaking. Overall, this mismatch highlights the need for more balanced development.</p>
</div>
