<td id="S4.T5.4.1.8.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;">6.46</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.4.1.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.4.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;">Step-Audio</td>
<td id="S4.T5.4.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;"><span id="S4.T5.4.1.9.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">33.2</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.4.1.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;"><span id="S4.T5.4.1.9.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">56.0</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.4.1.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;">90.5</td>
<td id="S4.T5.4.1.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;"><span id="S4.T5.4.1.9.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">75.1</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.4.1.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;"><span id="S4.T5.4.1.9.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">12.92</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Smaller, well-architected models can rival larger models.</span> As shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the performance of leading mid-sized models is often better than that of larger models. A standout example is Step-Audio-2-mini, which achieves more than double the listening accuracy of the 32B LLaMA-Omni2 model (40.06 vs. 16.00) and obtains the highest overall score.
Likewise, other mid-sized models such as Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and MiniCPM-o-2_6 perform competitively in many benchmarks, demonstrating that well-targeted training can unlock significant capabilities in relatively modest models.
Furthermore, large models tend to perform better on Speaking tasks but especially poorly on Listening tasks. This suggests that the bottleneck in audio understanding lies in the audio encoder, while the size of the LLM backbone primarily influences the quality of the generated speech.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Role-play tasks are challenging.</span> As shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the detailed role-play results expose a clear tension between content quality and audio naturalness. Step-Audio achieves the highest content score (33.2) and speaker similarity (75.1) but has the second lowest speech naturalness (56.0). In contrast, the smaller Step-Audio-2-mini model produces highly fluent speech (76.0) but has much weaker content quality (12.7). The implication is that developers of voice assistants must balance semantic accuracy with audio fidelity. Otherwise, a model might sound natural while conveying incorrect information, or be accurate in the speech content while sounding unnatural.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-modal (vision+audio) integration remains a challenge.</span> Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> highlights a substantial gap in model performance on the same questions when presented through different input modalities. All models perform significantly worse when given an image paired with spoken input compared to the same image with written text. For example, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B achieves 59.2% accuracy on image+text queries but only 42.9% on image + audio queries, with a 16.3-point drop. More broadly, this suggests that current voice assistant models can reason effectively about images when textual hints are provided, but they struggle to do so directly from speech. Bridging this gap will require either improved multi-modal architectures or more integrated training data, since true audio-visual understanding remains limited in today’s voice assistants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safety alignment and robustness still require further improvement.</span> The results in Tab. <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Models ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show that safety alignment and robustness vary widely across models, with some smaller or unaligned models, such as the Moshika family, nearly failing on both fronts (robustness below 1, safety below 28). In contrast, well-aligned models such as Freeze-Omni (safety 79.8) demonstrate that explicit alignment training and diverse data exposure substantially improve performance. For developers, this means that prioritizing suitable alignment methods and noise-robust training is critical to ensuring that voice assistants remain both trustworthy and reliable in everyday use.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Human Evaluation for Error Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides a qualitative analysis of the errors made by Qwen2.5-Omni-7B. We extract 593 error cases and carefully examine their underlying causes. Results are shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Human Evaluation for Error Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The model encounters specific difficulties across listening, speaking, and viewing tasks. Listening tasks are mostly hindered by the loss of audio context and basic perception errors. Speaking tasks show that the model typically understands the prompt but struggles to fully meet the requirements in terms of content, style, or emotion. Viewing task errors are dominated by the misrecognition and misinterpretation of visual input. Together, this breakdown highlights where Qwen2.5-Omni-7B falls short, pointing the way toward future improvements in audio memory, response generation, and visual understanding. Please refer to Appendix <a href="#A12" title="Appendix L Error Analysis Details ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></a> for more detailed examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Errors in Listening.</span>
For listening, the most frequent error is <em id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Context Loss Error</em>, which constitutes 46%. Here, the model loses track of earlier audio content when answering, sometimes even with relatively short inputs, essentially leading to hallucinated responses. The next largest categories are <em id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Speech Perception Error</em> (16%) and <em id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sound Perception Error</em> (15%), showing that the model also struggles to accurately recognize spoken words and distinguish between sounds. Taken together, nearly one-third of listening failures stem from problems in perceiving the audio input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Errors in Speaking.</span>
For speaking tasks, the error profile shifts toward output quality rather than perception. The most frequent categories are <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Insufficient Answer Error</em> (25%) and <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Requirement Deviation Error</em> (23%). These errors reveal that the model struggles to produce satisfactory answers, either omitting required content or diverging from the prompt. The third-largest category is <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Roleplay Style Error</em> (13%), where the model cannot maintain the requested persona or tone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Errors in Viewing.</span>
In vision-based tasks, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B’s greatest weakness is basic visual interpretation. <em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vision Perception Error</em> accounts for 50% of all viewing errors, as the model frequently misidentifies or overlooks key elements in an image. The next largest categories are <em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Knowledge Error</em> (19%) and <em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reasoning Error</em> (15%). In these cases, the model correctly perceives the image but applies incorrect factual knowledge or faulty reasoning. Finally, <em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vision Context Loss Error</em> (12%) occurs when the model incorrectly claims that no image is provided.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22651v1/errorana_pie.svg" id="S4.SS3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/183;" width="521" height="183"></object>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Error analysis of Qwen2.5-Omni-7B across listening, speaking, and viewing tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span>, the first large-scale benchmark that systematically evaluates voice assistants on integrated listening, speaking, and viewing tasks. Our extensive experiments show that while current models can generate fluent speech and handle straightforward conversational queries, they struggle with rich audio understanding and multimodal integration. In particular, most models perform better on speaking tasks than on listening tasks, and performance on joint audio–image queries is significantly lower than on text–image queries. We also find that smaller, specialized models can outperform larger models on certain tasks and that balancing response fidelity with voice naturalness remains a challenge in role-play scenarios. These outcomes highlight concrete directions for improvement: future research should focus on enhancing models’ audio perception and memory, strengthening multimodal understanding to close the gap between written and spoken inputs, and refining training and alignment methods to improve safety and robustness. Ultimately, <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> offers a rigorous foundation for measuring progress toward truly versatile, voice-enabled AI assistants, enabling transparent, longitudinal assessment of system capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Reproducibility Statement</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure reproducibility, we will release both the dataset and evaluation code used in this work. Comprehensive details of the evaluated models, including timestamps (where available) and generation configurations, are provided in Appendix <a href="#A11.SS1" title="K.1 Model Setup ‣ Appendix K Experiment Details ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K.1</span></a>. All evaluation prompts are listed in Appendix <a href="#A11.SS2" title="K.2 Evaluation Prompts ‣ Appendix K Experiment Details ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K.2</span></a>, and the sources of our data are specified in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Data Collection ‣ 3 Dataset ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, with further details in Tab. <a href="#A5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix E Source Datasets ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The data curation tool is described in Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Data Curation Tool ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>. Evaluation metrics, protocols, and relevant equations are outlined in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. To further support the reliability of our results, we present human evaluation validation (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Human Evaluation Validation ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>), demonstrate evaluation stability (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Stability of the Evaluation ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>), and report statistical significance testing (Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Statistical Significance Testing ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). Collectively, these resources are intended to ensure that our findings are transparent, robust, and independently verifiable.</p>
</div>
</section>
