<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Limitations and Future Work</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> offers a comprehensive evaluation across listening, speaking, and viewing, we acknowledge several limitations in the current benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, the dataset, though sizable (10.5K curated QA items), may still be limited in diversity. Although we include five Chinese roles in the role-play task, all other prompts and evaluations are in English. This monolingual focus may not capture the full variability of real user interactions. These constraints mean that model performance on our benchmark, while indicative, might not fully generalize to more diverse linguistic settings. Future iterations can expand the dataset’s diversity along multiple axes, incorporating non-English languages to better mirror global usage.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, the range of models evaluated in our study is limited. We focus primarily on open-source models, and due to cost and access constraints, we include only a single proprietary system (GPT-4o-Audio) in our experiments. This provides a narrow glimpse into how closed-source industry models compare with open ones on our benchmark. We hope that the community will contribute to future work by extending the benchmarking to a broader set of models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Third, our evaluation methodology has limitations. We rely on automated measures and GPT-based scoring, which, while efficient, may introduce noise and bias. The GPT judge may favor certain phrasings, overlook nuanced errors, or fail to capture all aspects of human preferences. Nevertheless, we demonstrate that our evaluation scheme aligns strongly with human judgments through thorough validation, and that our conclusions are reliable, supported by statistical significance testing. Future work could refine the evaluation with more robust automatic metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Fourth, like many benchmarks, <span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> may carry biases from its source data and design choices. The questions and contexts are drawn from academic or synthetic sources (e.g., existing datasets and rewritten instructions), which might not reflect the full richness or fairness concerns of real deployments. For example, the role-play subset uses a fixed set of 100 roles and associated style prompts. These roles may encode stereotypes or omit important user personas. Addressing bias and realism is a crucial direction for the future. We also encourage the community to analyze model outputs for biases or unsafe behavior on our tasks and to contribute additional test cases that probe fairness and ethical considerations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Fifth, although we span 13 task categories, there remain gaps in task coverage and scenario realism. For instance, while we introduce multi-modal queries (image + audio), video-audio integration and continuous audio-visual streaming tasks are beyond the scope of this benchmark. The absence of these scenarios means current evaluations might overestimate a model’s capabilities in controlled settings compared to messy, continuous real-world input. An important direction for future work is to broaden task coverage by adding dynamic and continuous interaction tasks, such as understanding live audio streams or video narratives. Introducing these more challenging, realistic scenarios would stress-test the model’s memory and adaptability, moving evaluation closer to real assistant behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, <span id="A1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> evaluates models in an offline, static manner, which might not capture certain practical deployment factors. Real-world voice assistants operate under latency constraints, continuous listening requirements, and sometimes involve incremental speech processing. Our current evaluation does not measure reaction time, the ability to handle partial utterances, or how well a model can manage barge-in (user interruption) and error recovery. Future work could explore an interactive evaluation framework, where models are tested in simulated real-time conversations or through user-in-the-loop studies to observe how they perform in a closed feedback loop.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, while we acknowledge certain limitations in our current approach, <span id="A1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> already offers a comprehensive and reliable framework for evaluating the listening, speaking, and viewing capabilities of AI assistants. Our results demonstrate that the benchmark is well-suited for rigorous testing and is valuable for both researchers and practitioners. Recognizing its current boundaries allows us and the broader community to target meaningful improvements. By expanding data coverage, refining evaluation metrics, and introducing new challenges, future work can further enhance <span id="A1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> as a robust proxy for real-world voice assistant performance. These ongoing efforts will help drive the development of more capable, fair, and versatile multi-modal AI systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Human Evaluation Validation</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To verify that our automatic evaluation scheme aligns with human preferences, we conduct a human evaluation of Qwen2.5-Omni-7B’s outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We randomly sampled 10% of the model’s responses from each of the 13 tasks, resulting in 1050 examples. These responses are then independently evaluated by human judges.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix B Human Evaluation Validation ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a very strong agreement between our automated model-based evaluation scheme (using gpt-oss-20b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) and human judgments across various tasks. All reported Pearson correlation coefficients between the model’s scores and human ratings are notably high (ranging from 0.73 to 1.00, with an overall correlation of 0.92), indicating a strong linear relationship. For example, several categories achieve a perfect correlation of 1.00, meaning our model’s ranking of outputs exactly matches the human rankings in those cases. Even the lowest observed correlation (0.73 for robustness tests, RBT) remains well above zero, indicating considerable alignment even in the most challenging category. These consistently high correlations demonstrate that our content-quality metric (derived from gpt-oss-20b) reliably reflects human perceptions of response quality across both listening and speaking tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Agreement rate indicates the percentage of examples in which our model’s evaluation matches that of the human evaluators. These rates are uniformly high (often above 90%), with several categories at a perfect 100%, meaning that in nearly all cases the model’s evaluation concurs with the human evaluation. The overall agreement rate is 96.1%. In practical terms, this means that if human evaluators judge certain outputs to meet quality standards (or not), our automated scheme almost always reaches the same conclusion. Such strong agreement further confirms the reliability of our evaluation scheme. Taken together, the high correlation coefficients and agreement rates clearly demonstrate that our automated scoring and consistency judgments closely align with human preferences, validating the scheme’s effectiveness as a proxy for human evaluation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Pearson correlation coefficients and agreement rates between gpt-oss-20b and human evaluations across task categories on <span id="A2.T6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span>. Listening Tasks: GEN: General, MUS: Music, SND: Sound, SPH: Speech. Speaking Tasks: AST: Assistant, EMO: Emotion, IF: Instruction Following, MR: Multi-Round, RSN: Reasoning, RBT: Robustness, RLP: Roleplay, SFT: Safety. Viewing Task: MDS: Multi-Discipline. The highest scores among open-source models are highlighted in <span id="A2.T6.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">red</span>, the second-highest scores in <span id="A2.T6.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D2E6FA;">blue</span>, and the third-highest in <span id="A2.T6.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCF4E5;">green</span>.</figcaption>
