</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Stability of the Evaluation</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To rigorously assess the stability of our automatic evaluation scheme, we repeat the scoring process ten times for each task and quantified the variance in the resulting scores. For this analysis, we randomly sample 10% of the Qwen2.5-Omni-7B’s responses from each of the 13 tasks, yielding 1,050 examples in total.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results (see Fig. <a href="#A3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix C Stability of the Evaluation ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) demonstrate that the evaluation scores are highly consistent across repeated runs. For example, the Listening Sound task receive an identical score of 61.5 in every run (variance = 0, standard deviation = 0.0). For the Listening Music task, the scores vary only minimally, with nine runs yielding 26.7 and one run 25.0 (mean = 26.5, standard deviation = 0.5).
Among the spoken-response tasks, Speaking Roleplay scores range from 2.6 to 5.4 (mean = 4.1, standard deviation = 0.8), indicating low variability. The task with the largest observed spread, Speaking Robustness, produce scores from 44.8 to 51.0 (mean = 47.8, standard deviation = 2.3).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These findings confirm that our evaluation methodology is highly stable: repeated assessments yield nearly identical scores for every task. The narrow box widths in Fig. <a href="#A3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix C Stability of the Evaluation ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (often enclosing only 1–2 points), together with low standard deviations (mostly below 3), provide strong evidence that the automated scores are both reproducible and reliable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22651v1/acc_box.svg" id="A3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/204;" width="548" height="204"></object>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Stability of automated evaluation across repeated runs. Boxplots show the distribution of the Qwen2.5-Omni-7B’s scores for each task over ten evaluation runs. The red line indicates the median, each box represents the interquartile range (IQR), and whiskers extend to the full range of observed scores. The narrow IQRs confirm that our automated evaluation produces stable, repeatable results.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Statistical Significance Testing</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To rigorously validate the performance differences observed in our benchmark, we carry out statistical significance tests between representative models. Specifically, we compare Qwen2.5-Omni-7B against LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual across the same evaluation samples. We randomly sample 10% of the dataset from each of the 13 tasks (1,050 examples total) and repeat the scoring process ten times. For each metric, we report the mean and standard deviation of the scores from these runs. We then use paired two-sided t-tests (via scipy.stats.ttest_rel) on the matched score distributions, applying a Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons.
Tab. <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix D Statistical Significance Testing ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> summarizes the results. In this table, the “<math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>” column shows the difference in average score for each task, and the “Sig.” column indicates whether the difference is statistically significant at the corrected level (<math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\checkmark" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>✓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\checkmark</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.0042" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.0042</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.0042</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the Listening tasks, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B consistently outperforms LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual by large margins. For example, on Listening Sound, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B scores <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="61.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>61.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">61.5</annotation></semantics></math> vs. LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual’s <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.0</annotation></semantics></math>, a huge gain of +51.5 points. Similarly, on Listening Music, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B scores <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>26.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26.5</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.9</annotation></semantics></math> (+19.6), and on Listening General, <math id="A4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>31.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31.9</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22.9</annotation></semantics></math> (+9.0). All of these differences are highly significant (<math id="A4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math> after correction). The average listening accuracy improves from <math id="A4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.5\pm 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.5\pm 0.6</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40.5\pm 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40.5\pm 0.6</annotation></semantics></math>, a +21.0 point gain (t = 79.50, <math id="A4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). In fact, every listening task shows a statistically significant improvement for Qwen2.5-Omni-7B, confirming that the smaller model has substantially better audio understanding under our evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Speaking tasks show a more nuanced pattern. Some tasks favor Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and others favor LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual, but the overall differences are smaller. For Speaking Assistant, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B scores <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66.3\pm 2.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>66.3</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66.3\pm 2.4</annotation></semantics></math> vs LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual’s <math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="68.1\pm 2.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>68.1</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">68.1\pm 2.3</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> = –1.8). This difference is not statistically significant (<math id="A4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.117" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.117</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.117</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating comparable performance. On the other hand, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B significantly outperforms LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual on tasks like Speaking Emotion (36.7 vs 25.0, <math id="A4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> = +11.7, <math id="A4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>) and Speaking Robustness (47.8 vs 34.6, <math id="A4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> = +13.2, <math id="A4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). Conversely, LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual does better on Speaking Instruction Following (37.7 vs 31.9, LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual +5.7, <math id="A4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>) and Speaking Safety (80.3 vs 78.0, +2.3, <math id="A4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.003" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.003</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.003</annotation></semantics></math>, which remains significant after correction). On Speaking Multi-Round, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B’s 72.7 vs LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual’s 68.4 yields <math id="A4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> = +4.2 (t=2.81), but the raw <math id="A4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.020" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.020</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.020</annotation></semantics></math> does not meet the Bonferroni threshold, so we consider it non-significant. There is effectively no difference on Speaking Reasoning (67.0 vs 67.4, <math id="A4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> = –0.4, <math id="A4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.634" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.634</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.634</annotation></semantics></math>). In summary, out of eight speaking categories, five show statistically significant differences (marked ✓), while three are non-significant (marked ✗). The overall speaking-average scores are 50.6 (Qwen2.5-Omni-7B) and 47.7 (LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual), a modest +2.9 difference that is statistically significant (t = 8.86, <math id="A4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>) but much smaller than the listening gap.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In practical terms, these tests confirm that the observed performance gaps are not due to chance. The Bonferroni-corrected significance level (0.0042) ensures we only flag very strong effects. The large t-statistics and very low p-values for most listening tasks (and several speaking tasks) indicate highly reliable differences. For example, the t-stat for Listening Sound is effectively infinite (since model’s score was constant across runs), leading to <math id="A4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-10}</annotation></semantics></math>. The smaller t-statistics in the speaking tasks reflect either small score gaps or higher variation between runs.
From these results, we see clearly that Qwen2.5-Omni-7B’s advantages in audio understanding are statistically confirmed, while differences in speaking abilities are generally smaller and mixed in direction. This fortifies our earlier claim: smaller, carefully trained models can rival or surpass larger ones on certain tasks, and large models tend to perform better on Speaking tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>The statistical comparison between Qwen2.5-Omni-7B nd LLaMA-Omni2-32B-Bilingual </figcaption>
<div id="A4.T7.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:163.2pt;vertical-align:-79.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-52.5pt,21.6pt) scale(0.7908840582139,0.7908840582139) ;">
