<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Dataset Intended Usage and License</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We document the <span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> dataset within the paper and note that the <span id="A6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> and code for result reproduction will be publicly available. The <span id="A6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> dataset is not supposed to be used, though the risk exists, for training models for cheating. We intend for researchers to use this dataset to better evaluate voice assistants and, consequently, facilitate future studies in this area. We bear all responsibility in case of violation of rights. The <span id="A6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span> data and our open-source code are under an MIT license.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, because the benchmark includes role-play voice imitation and speaker similarity evaluation, we emphasize strict consent, provenance, and anti-impersonation safeguards. To mitigate misuse risks (e.g., identity spoofing, harassment, fraud), we (i) prohibit training or releasing models intended to reproduce identifiable speakers without documented consent; (ii) require downstream users to implement consent gating and watermarking or traceability of synthetic audio; (iii) provide “safety cards” that describe acceptable use, known risks, and red-team tests for voice cloning abuse. We will maintain a data governance process for auditing and removal and reserve the right to update assets if consent or licensing concerns arise. These measures aim to enable research on personalization while minimizing harms related to impersonation, privacy violations, and unequal performance across demographics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Detailed Assessment of Model Capabilities</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The comprehensive evaluation of modern voice assistant models across the critical dimensions of content quality (Tab.<a href="#A7.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix G Detailed Assessment of Model Capabilities ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), speech quality (Tab.<a href="#A7.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix G Detailed Assessment of Model Capabilities ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), and text-to-speech consistency (Tab. <a href="#A7.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Appendix G Detailed Assessment of Model Capabilities ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) provides a detailed snapshot of the current technological landscape. By analyzing the performance of both proprietary and open-source models on a wide array of listening, speaking, and viewing tasks, we can discern distinct patterns, inherent trade-offs, and areas of both significant advancement and persistent challenges. The following section delves into five key insights derived from these experimental results, each highlighting a crucial aspect of model behavior with important implications for the future development and deployment of voice assistant technology.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o-Audio excels in content but lags in speech quality.</span>
The proprietary GPT-4o-Audio model leads by a wide margin in content quality for complex tasks but lags behind open-source models in speech quality and consistency. For example, in the Speaking Assistant task, GPT-4o scores 90.2 in content, which is 24.9 points higher than the best open model (MiniCPM-o-2_6 at 65.3), showcasing its advanced language understanding and generation. However, its spoken delivery in that task earns a score of 76 for speech naturalness, whereas the best open-source speech model, Step-Audio-2-mini, scores around 90. Its content-speech consistency is 91.4, which is 5.5 points lower than the top open-source consistency score (Qwen2.5-Omni’s 96.9). This disparity indicates that GPT-4o’s strength lies more in its language capabilities than in its voice. It can formulate excellent answers, but it does not sound as clear or lifelike as some smaller models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open-source models currently lead in speech naturalness.</span>
We find that strong open-source models consistently produce more natural speech than GPT-4o-Audio. Step-Audio-2-mini achieves the highest quality ratings (mostly in the 90s) for speech quality, as shown in Tab. <a href="#A7.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix G Detailed Assessment of Model Capabilities ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, whereas GPT-4o’s speech scores are in the low to mid-80s. This 10–14 point gap is consistent across tasks such as Speaking Assistant and Speaking Emotion. In industry terms, this means open TTS technologies are currently more fluent and human-like than GPT-4o’s out-of-the-box voice. Encouragingly, even smaller or midsize public models match or exceed GPT-4o’s audio output. This democratization of high-fidelity TTS suggests that deploying a new voice assistant need not rely on proprietary models for high sound quality. Open models suffice or even excel in this area, potentially reducing costs and increasing transparency.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A Trade-off Exists Between Speech Naturalness and Content Consistency.</span>
There is a clear trade-off in the open-source models between generating highly fluent, natural-sounding speech and ensuring that the spoken output is perfectly faithful to the intended text. This is exemplified by comparing Step-Audio-2-mini with the Qwen series. Step-Audio-2-mini consistently achieves the highest speech quality scores, often reaching 90, but its consistency scores are lower, such as 77.9 in the Speaking Assistant task. Conversely, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and its 3B variant dominate in consistency, with scores frequently in the high 90s (e.g., 96.9 in AST), but their speech quality scores are lower, typically in the mid-80s. This suggests that developers must prioritize either a more pleasant user experience or absolute precision, highlighting the need for new methods that can deliver both high-quality speech and unwavering consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-domain strengths vary by model specialization.</span>
Our results highlight that no model dominates all task categories. Open audio-specialist models often lead on listening tasks, while GPT-4o excels at language-intensive speaking tasks. For instance, Step-Audio-2-mini and other open models score highest on music and sound recognition prompts, whereas GPT-4o falls slightly behind. GPT-4o’s content scores on listening sound (57.9) and listening speech (46.7) tasks lag behind the best open models by 2.9 and 10.1 points, respectively. Conversely, GPT-4o outperforms open models on multi-turn, reasoning, and instruction-following tasks. This suggests a design implication: voice assistants may benefit from hybrid architectures that route audio inputs through dedicated perception models and dialogue through powerful LLM back-ends. In other words, models trained specifically on audio (sound, music, or speech recognition) currently have an edge in those niches, whereas GPT-4o’s strength lies in higher-level semantic and discourse processing.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scale and specialization both matter unevenly.</span> Larger models have clear content advantages in many tasks, but they do not automatically yield better voice outputs. For example, MiniCPM-o-2_6 scores highest among open models on the Speaking Assistant task (65.3 content) but still trails GPT-4o by 24.9 points. Conversely, a 130B Step-Audio model produces worse speech quality than the much smaller Step-Audio-2-mini. This indicates that larger parameter counts are most valuable for complex reasoning and understanding (where GPT-4o leads), while speech quality often depends more on architecture and data targeted at audio. The fact that open-source models of modest size can rival or exceed large proprietary ones in certain metrics highlights the importance of targeted training: scaling up helps comprehension, but human-like voices likely come from fine-tuning on speech data and audio-centric design.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Models’ content scores on <span id="A7.T9.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VoiceAssistant-Eval</span>. Listening Tasks: GEN: General, MUS: Music, SND: Sound, SPH: Speech. Speaking Tasks: AST: Assistant, EMO: Emotion, IF: Instruction Following, MR: Multi-Round, RSN: Reasoning, RBT: Robustness, RLP: Roleplay, SFT: Safety. Viewing Task: MDS: Multi-Discipline. The highest scores among open-source models are highlighted in <span id="A7.T9.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">red</span>, the second-highest scores in <span id="A7.T9.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D2E6FA;">blue</span>, and the third-highest in <span id="A7.T9.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCF4E5;">green</span>.</figcaption>
