</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Usage of Large Language Models in Paper Writing</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is primarily authored and composed by humans, with the core ideas, research, and analysis being conducted independently by the authors. Nevertheless, large language models such as o3-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>, GPT-4.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and DeepSeek-V3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> are utilized as auxiliary tools during the writing process. Specifically, these advanced language models are employed to review the manuscript for grammatical errors, spelling mistakes, and typographical inconsistencies. In addition to basic proofreading, these models occasionally assist in refining certain words and phrases, contributing to greater clarity and fluency throughout the text. Importantly, although LLMs provide suggestions for wording and stylistic refinement, all substantive content, arguments, and conclusions are the authors’ own. The involvement of language models is thus limited to surface-level linguistic improvements, ensuring that the intellectual contributions remain wholly human-driven.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A9" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix I </span>Detailed Related Work</h2>

<section id="A9.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">I.1 </span>Speech Interaction Benchmarks.</h3>

<div id="A9.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent benchmarks have begun to target the end-to-end conversation ability of audio-based LLMs. For example, <span id="A9.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VoiceBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> is one of the first comprehensive benchmarks for LLM-based voice assistants. It includes both real and synthetic spoken instructions under diverse realistic conditions (variable accents, noise, etc.) and evaluates multiple facets such as general knowledge, instruction following, and safety. VoiceBench uses synthesized speech to prompt the model but assesses the content of its textual responses, highlighting the limitations of current voice assistants (e.g., pipeline models vs. end-to-end models).</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A related effort, <span id="A9.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VocalBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, explicitly focuses on vocal conversational ability. VocalBench provides around 9.4K test instances spanning four dimensions: semantic quality, acoustic performance (e.g., the naturalness of the generated voice), conversational skills, and robustness to noise, covering 16 fundamental interaction skills. By measuring both what is said and how it is said, VocalBench reveals varied strengths across models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Likewise, <span id="A9.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SOVA-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2025</a>)</cite> extends evaluation to include speech output quality. SOVA-Bench is organized into multiple tasks: general knowledge Q&amp;A, spoken question answering, emotion/gender/age recognition, and voice response generation. In particular, its generation module tests a model’s ability to produce natural spoken replies: on top of checking semantic consistency with a reference answer, it scores acoustic quality using learned MOS predictors. This is unique among benchmarks in explicitly quantifying the sound of the response (prosody, clarity, etc.) rather than only its transcribed content.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SD-Eval</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> takes a complementary approach by emphasizing spoken dialogue understanding beyond words. It assembles 7.3K real utterances (about 8.7 hours of audio) drawn from existing corpora, each labeled with one of four paralinguistic or environmental categories (emotion, accent, speaker age, background noise). SD-Eval’s focus on raw speech inputs and nuanced attributes is uncommon; most earlier valve-like evaluations ignore these supra-segmental features. A limitation is that the current SD-Eval release is “speech-to-text” (evaluating generated text replies) rather than full speech-to-speech dialogue, but it paves the way for more realistic spoken interaction tests.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, <span id="A9.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WildSpeech-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> explicitly targets natural, multi-turn speech conversations. It curates authentic dialogue snippets and question–answer pairs from real chat logs (with diverse speakers, recording conditions, etc.) and deliberately injects speech-specific phenomena such as prosodic emphasis shifts, homophones, hesitations, and stuttering. WildSpeech-Bench then uses a “query-aware” automated evaluation protocol: customized prompts and checklists guide a GPT-4 mini judge to assess model replies for correctness under these nuanced conditions. This benchmark is unique in focusing on everyday speech quirks that can trip up an LLM (e.g., identifying the intended meaning of a sentence given altered stress). Early analyses using WildSpeech-Bench have shown pronounced performance gaps between models when handling these phenomena. However, as with VoiceBench, current evaluations concentrate on content accuracy using text output (rather than scoring actual synthesized speech), so they measure the practical impact of speech challenges but not the acoustic rendering itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A9.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">I.2 </span>Audio and Vision Understanding Benchmarks.</h3>

<div id="A9.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A variety of datasets support audio–text and visual–text tasks, each with its own role.
<span id="A9.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LibriSpeech</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panayotov et al., 2015</a>)</cite> is a standard ASR corpus of approximately 1,000 hours of read English speech from audiobooks. Its clean, well-aligned transcripts have made it foundational for training and benchmarking speech recognition and related LLM modules.
<span id="A9.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mozilla Common Voice</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ardila et al., 2020</a>)</cite> is a crowdsourced, multi-domain speech corpus covering dozens of languages and accents. With thousands of hours and tens of thousands of speakers, Common Voice is widely used to evaluate ASR and multi-lingual transfer in audio-LMs.
<span id="A9.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Clotho</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Drossos et al., 2020</a>)</cite> contains approximately 4.9K real-world audio clips (8–30 s each) paired with crowdsourced captions (25K total). It is specifically created for audio captioning (describing soundscapes in text) and has been used to train models that generate free-form summaries of environmental sounds.
<span id="A9.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AudioCaps</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2019</a>)</cite> is similar but larger: about 46K short audio clips from YouTube, each with at least one human-written caption. These benchmarks have catalyzed research in audio-to-text generation. Limitations include a moderate size and domain bias (mostly domestic or nature sounds, with no verbatim speech content).</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-modal question-answering adds visual context. <span id="A9.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AVQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is a large-scale Video-QA dataset with approximately 57K real-world clips and 57K QAs. In AVQA, each question explicitly requires both the audio and visual signals from the clip to answer correctly (e.g., “What instrument is playing while the person in blue gestures?”). AVQA’s key features are that it is not restricted to scripted environments, clips depict daily activities, and it has dedicated models (e.g., hierarchical fusion modules) tested on it. Similarly, <span id="A9.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MUSIC-AVQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">li et al., 2022</a>)</cite> focuses on musical performances: it contains about 45.9K QA pairs across 9.3K video clips (about 150 h total) of people playing instruments. Questions in MUSIC-AVQA probe musical concepts (instrument identity, tempo changes, etc.) that often require both audio and visual understanding. Both AVQA and MUSIC-AVQA have been used to benchmark multimodal LLMs; ablation studies show how performance degrades when either audio or video is removed, highlighting their necessity. A limitation is that these datasets emphasize short video clips (10–20 s) and specific domains (everyday scenes or music), so they cover only part of the audio–visual space.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond specific tasks, there are broader audio representation benchmarks. <span id="A9.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SUPERB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>)</cite> is a unified evaluation suite for speech-related tasks. It aggregates a diverse set of tasks (phoneme recognition, speaker ID, keyword spotting, etc.) under a single “frozen features” framework: a model’s learned speech embeddings are fixed, and only lightweight task heads are trained on top. SUPERB has been widely adopted: virtually every self-supervised audio model (Wav2Vec2, HuBERT, Whisper, etc.) reports SUPERB scores to demonstrate generality across tasks. However, it focuses on discriminative benchmarks (mainly classification or ASR), not on generative or open-ended interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="A9.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HEAR benchmark</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baur et al., 2024</a>)</cite> complements this by treating representation evaluation more holistically. It evaluates audio embeddings on 19 diverse downstream tasks (spanning speech, environmental sound, and music recognition) without any fine-tuning. The goal is to find one encoder that works “like the human ear” across domains. HEAR’s long-term value is that it standardizes many small tasks (gender ID, note detection, noise classification, etc.) into a single scoring framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, <span id="A9.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AIR-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> explicitly targets audio-language models in a generative setting. It consists of two parts: a “foundation” benchmark of about 19K multiple-choice questions on pure audio understanding (speech, sound, or music stimuli), and a “chat” benchmark of about 2K open-ended QA pairs requiring free-form answers to audio queries. Importantly, AIR-Bench requires the model to generate answers (not just select or transcribe), and it uses an automated GPT-4-based evaluator to score these generations. In experiments, GPT-based scoring on AIR-Bench aligned well with human judgment. AIR-Bench is unique in being the first large-scale test of end-to-end audio-to-text generation. Like other audio benchmarks, its limitation is that it still only tests the quality of textual output; audio-LMs with speech output are evaluated via their transcription.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond audio, a few benchmarks combine vision and audio for advanced reasoning. <span id="A9.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMMU</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a very challenging multi-disciplinary test: it contains 11.5K multimodal questions drawn from college-level exams across six domains (Science, Humanities, Medicine, Engineering, etc.). The accompanying images are extremely varied, including charts, maps, diagrams, music scores, chemical formulas, etc. So MMMU stresses not only cross-modal perception but also deep domain knowledge. In evaluations released by the authors, even GPT-4V and Gemini Ultra scored only around <math id="A9.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>56</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A9.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>59</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy on MMMU, significantly below human performance. Its size and breadth make MMMU a touchstone for “expert” reasoning in multimodal models, but training on it or closely related data is nontrivial.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Two recent benchmarks target math reasoning in images specifically. <span id="A9.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MathVista</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> assembles 6,141 math problems that require visual understanding (e.g., geometry shapes, plots, tables). Problems are drawn from 28 existing multimodal math datasets plus three new collections. MathVista tests skills like algebra, geometry, number theory in a visual context. Evaluations on MathVista show that even strong LMMs struggle on complex diagram questions (best accuracy about 50% vs. about 60% human). <span id="A9.SS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MATH-Vision</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> similarly evaluates mathematical reasoning; it provides 3,040 high-quality problems from real competitions, spanning 16 topics and 5 difficulty levels. By design, MATH-Vision is narrower (only math) but deeper, enabling fine-grained analysis of where models fail (e.g., calculus vs. geometry). Both MathVista and MATH-Vision highlight that current LMMs, while strong in language, are far from reliable on visually-presented math. They represent the cutting edge of multimodal benchmarks, pointing out avenues for integrating symbolic and perceptual reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A10" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix J </span>Data Curation Tool</h2>

<div id="A10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To operationalize the validation step described in the main text, we build a lightweight web-based curation tool that standardizes quality checks across modalities. The tool ingests candidate items consisting of a unique ID, metadata, raw prompt, reference answer(s), and associated media (audio waveform(s), image(s), and optional multi-turn transcripts). Curators review each item on a single screen, with inline players and viewers, transcripts, and multiple-choice options when present, and then assign a Good or Bad verdict, along with optional notes. The tool exports an audit log with item IDs, curator decisions, and timestamps; approved items are written to a clean JSONL shard used for evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A10.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#A10.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix J Data Curation Tool ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>–<a href="#A10.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix J Data Curation Tool ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> illustrate representative views: Fig.<a href="#A10.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix J Data Curation Tool ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a listening task with user and stimulus audio players, plus transcripts; Fig.<a href="#A10.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix J Data Curation Tool ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows a multi-round speaking task with three user audio clips and evolving prompts; Fig.<a href="#A10.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix J Data Curation Tool ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows a role-play speaking task with both user and role audio; Fig.<a href="#A10.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix J Data Curation Tool ‣ VoiceAssistant-Eval: Benchmarking AI Assistants across Listening, Speaking, and Viewing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows a multi-discipline viewing task with the image, options, and reference answer.
Manual review proceeds in three quick passes: (1) Media integrity: Verify that the audio and images are present, decodable, audible, and visible, and that they are not silent or corrupted; (2) Task well-posedness: Confirm that the prompt is clear, unambiguous, and that inputs align with the stated task (e.g., options match the question); (3) Answer validity: Check that reference answers are consistent with the prompt and media, and that multi-turn items are coherent. Approximately 10% of candidates are discarded via this process.</p>
</div>
