<span id="A12.SS2.p3.pic1.2.12.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:355.9pt;">
<span id="A12.SS2.p3.pic1.2.12.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A12.SS2.p3.pic1.2.12.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">&lt;analysis&gt;
We need to evaluate: The question: "Which emotion do you feel when you listen to this piece of music? Press A for fun, B for Christmas, C for fast, or D for relaxing." Reference answer: "christmas" (that’s option B). The Model Answer: "Well, I’d say it’s D, relaxing…". So the model answer says D instead of B. That does not match. So it’s Incorrect.</span></span>
<span id="A12.SS2.p3.pic1.2.12.1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A12.SS2.p3.pic1.2.12.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">&lt;assistantfinal&gt; [Incorrect]</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</table></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
        
<figure id="A12.F28" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 28: </span>Sound-perception error in the Listening Music task. The prompt asks for A: fun, B: Christmas, C: fast, or D: relaxing. The reference label is “Christmas” (B), but the model selects “relaxing” (D), misinterpreting the musical cues.</figcaption>
</figure>
      
</div>
